{%- doc -%}
  Works out which badge a product wears, from its tags.

  The rules live in one theme setting — `Name = colour`, one per line — so a shop
  runs its badges from a single list rather than from a field on every product.
  Tagging is something a merchant already does, and often already has done: the
  products in this store carry tags like "Custom Treats" and "gift box" written
  in whatever casing the day called for, so the match ignores case.

  The first line that matches wins, which makes the order of that list the order
  of precedence. A product tagged both Bestsellers and New shows whichever the
  merchant put higher, and they change their mind by dragging a line, not by
  editing products.

  The name in the rule is what the card shows, not the tag as the product spells
  it — so "bestsellers" on the product still reads "Bestsellers" on the card.

  Outputs `Name|colour`, or nothing at all when no rule matches. Liquid has no
  way to hand back two values, and a snippet that rendered the badge itself would
  put the card's own positioning outside the card; so the caller splits on the
  pipe. Both callers do it in two lines.

  @param {object} product - The product to read tags from.

  @example
  {%- capture badge -%}{% render 'ui-product-badge', product: product %}{%- endcapture -%}
  {%- assign badge_parts = badge | strip | split: '|' -%}
  {% render 'ui-product-card', badge: badge_parts[0], badge_tone: badge_parts[1] %}
{%- enddoc -%}

{%- liquid
  assign found = ''

  assign rules = settings.badge_rules | newline_to_br | split: '<br />'

  for rule in rules
    if found != ''
      break
    endif

    assign parts = rule | split: '='
    if parts.size < 2
      continue
    endif

    assign rule_name = parts[0] | strip
    assign rule_tone = parts[1] | strip | downcase
    if rule_name == blank or rule_tone == blank
      continue
    endif

    assign wanted = rule_name | downcase

    for tag in product.tags
      assign candidate = tag | strip | downcase
      if candidate == wanted
        assign found = rule_name | append: '|' | append: rule_tone
        break
      endif
    endfor
  endfor

  echo found
-%}
